    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Gunnar Henderson** and **Drake Baldwin** headlining our core, representing the most stable high-floor options. **Gunnar Henderson** carries a massive 70.72 efficiency rating, anchoring the middle infield even without a confirmed opponent, while **Drake Baldwin** is projected for a strong 70.50 outing against the favorable matchup of Tomoyuki Sugano. **Heliot Ramos** also clears the \"Strong\" threshold at 61.98; his upside against the platoon advantage provided by Patrick Sandoval makes him a critical piece of our outfield production, particularly given his recent form. We are leaning heavily into these high-efficiency spots to compensate for the volatility inherent in our lower-tier starters.\n\nWhile **Jackson Merrill** is a quality asset, he was the closest cut from the starting lineup, missing the cut with a 46.71 score. The optimizer favored **Nasim Nunez** and **Liam Hicks** due to specific lineup positioning and a preference for the defensive/speed utility that keeps **Nasim Nunez** relevant in our scarcity-focused build. We are monitoring the weather closely as we approach first pitch; **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.**, **Luis Robert Jr.**, and **Heliot Ramos** all face a 36-39% rain risk. Should these games be delayed or pushed, we lose critical at-bats in a category-sensitive environment. \n\nOur bench depth remains restricted by the ongoing absences of **Andy Pages**, **Jordan Westburg**, and **Jacob Wilson**, all of whom remain on the IL. For today, the primary objective is maintaining our SB output while navigating the high-xERA matchup for **Drake Baldwin** and **Dominic Smith** against Tomoyuki Sugano. We are avoiding the temptation to over-tinker with our bench, trusting the ATC projections to guide the daily output while we hope **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** (66.92) can provide the necessary pop to keep us competitive across the board.",
